in March 1936 Dorothea Lange was driving  through Nipomo California on her way  home after a long day of work for the  resettlement administration a New Deal  agency created by FDR to relocate  struggling families onto land developed  by the government Lange was a  photographer for the last 15 years or so  she made her living taking portraits of  the San Francisco delete' but after the  Great Depression hit she left her studio  and began to document the effects of the  crisis on residents of the city on  seeing those photographs Roy Stryker  immediately hired her to work for the  government on a project that would  involve documenting poor rural workers  in a propaganda effort to elicit  political support for government aid  Lange was riding home having finished  her work film ready to be sent back to  Washington when she passed a fee Pickers  camp pea picker was a name given to poor  migrants from Eastern cities and towns  who moved West often to California  looking for farm work during the  Depression at this camp  an early morning frost had killed the  crops so some 3,000 people were left in  their makeshift homes with no work some  starving but lying passed them by  exhausted from the day's work it was 20  more miles before she turned around she  didn't stay at the camp long in about  ten minutes she would be back on the  road again but not before taking one of  the most iconic pictures in the history  of photography here's how it happened  on arriving at the camp Lange is  immediately drawn as if by a magnet she  said to a family huddled under a tent  this is likely a test shot there are a  lot of things that make this composition  unpersuasive only the child in the  middle for example is offering her face  to the camera the oldest daughter on the  chair doesn't seem to notice the camera  at all and the mother turns away clearly  defensive not wanting her picture taken  so Lange talks to them as she's learned  to do after working this beat for a long  time and she helps the family arrange  themselves this composition is  definitely more pleasing than the last  but something's still not quite right  and it has to do with the eldest  daughter  year remember these photos have a  purpose to evoke sympathy from voters  that would translate into political  support as James Curtis points out  having an older daughter as well as  three younger ones could suggest to some  that this woman wasn't taking the right  precautions and family planning and was  less deserving of support or in other  words less like them so lang cuts her  off she captures or stages a breast  feeding scene playing on the religious  symbolism of the nursing Madonna but the  picture may have been too extreme for  viewers of that time what's more it was  clearly making the mother uncomfortable  and Lang is starting to realize that she  is the key to the scene specifically her  face so she alters the ankle asks one of  the children to come back in and you can  see the woman easing up but the older  child looks a bit awkward and the post  is blocking part of the baby's head so  lang tries again this time having the  great idea to include the trunk at an  angle and the foreground with the empty  peyten compelling imagery of the  family's struggle and nomadic lifestyle  the child looks wistful the baby is  sleeping and the mother has the right  expression - this is an extraordinary  photograph you can see Lange coming to  it through a series of adjustments it  achieves the objectives of her project  while being aesthetically powerful  it's exactly what Ron striker would have  loved to see but Lange thought she could  do one better this is the photograph you  probably know I don't think you can  really appreciate it until you  understand the ten minutes that came  before a lesser photographer would have  milked the children's faces for their  sympathetic potential Lange even tried  to do that herself but by the time she  arrives here she knows exactly what the  focus needs to be the daughters faces  turn behind their mothers shoulders  communicates that message of family a  family of the right sized mind you  without taking away from the central  face or the eyes which seemed at last to  let down their guard as they search the  distance and worry the face is so  important that Lange has the woman put  her hand on her chin so that her arm  points to it it's a technique that she's  used before anything that took attention  away from the face would hurt the  power of the image which also explains  why Lang had a darkroom assistant  removed the woman's other thumb here  where she was holding the post so that  she could keep her other hand to her  chin all this manipulation of course  speaks to the fact that there is very  little spontaneous in this iconic image  of so-called documentary photography  it's supposed to communicate a candid  truth about the moment but this is  anything but candid whether that  diminishes its power is up to you for me  being able to actually see the steps of  Lang's craft enhances her work it gives  the photograph a third dimension almost  as soon as it was developed this photo  took on a life of its own after it was  published in a San Francisco newspaper  the government sent 20,000 pounds of  food to the migrant camp at Nipomo  stryker  fell in love with it and sent it all  across the country after which it came  to define the depression in the American  consciousness and on a deeper level  it became the archetypal image of  struggling families in any era a second  thought a skilled photographer a mother  her kids and ten minutes in 1936 that  will live forever next time you have an  instinct to turn around listen to it hey  everybody welcome to 2019 thank you so  much for watching this episode was  brought to you by Nord VPN if you care  like I do about your privacy and  security online you should absolutely be  using a VPN or virtual private network  to keep your data safe by allowing you  to conne